\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Hole injection from metal contacts into few-layer InSe is poorly understood. We simulate it for back-gated six-layer $\gamma$-InSe with top and edge contacts, using an open-source drift-diffusion model built on DEVSIM. It includes Fermi--Dirac statistics, anisotropic transport, thermionic emission across the van der Waals gap, nonlocal tunnelling with an anisotropic hole mass and two-band dispersion, and self-heating, benchmarked on Au/MoS$_2$. With the measured pinning of direct metal/InSe contacts ($S=0.05$), every common metal gives a hole barrier of about 1.1~eV, crossed only by gate-induced tunnelling. Depinning is the first lever: a measured oxide interlayer lowers the Pt barrier to 0.52~eV and the resistance by orders of magnitude. Which geometry is then better depends on the remaining barrier, the hole-mass anisotropy and the film thickness, none yet measured for InSe. Top contacts tunnel along the $c$ axis, where holes are light, and edge contacts in-plane, where they are heavy. A phase diagram over both masses shows that top contacts win only when the in-plane/$c$-axis mass ratio exceeds a threshold, set by the ratio of the two barriers' WKB action integrals, that rises from 2--3 at the measured direct pinning (1.05~eV) to 11--12 at the interlayer barrier (0.52~eV), and is not reached at 0.25~eV. For the band masses of InSe (ratio 5--12), edge contacts are up to 12 times better after depinning and top contacts 13--44 times better with direct pinning; thicker films favour edges. The rule applies to any layered semiconductor; measuring the $c$-axis tunnelling mass would settle the choice.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} indium selenide; Schottky-barrier FET; contact resistance; tunnelling; electro-thermal simulation; van der Waals contacts

\linenumbers

\section{Introduction}
InSe combines a high electron mobility \cite{refband,refsuch,reffeng14}, ballistic short-channel transistors \cite{refjiang} and wafer-scale growth \cite{refqin}. Its transistors are nevertheless almost always n-type. Their mobility depends on the condition of the InSe surface and of its dielectric interface \cite{refho,reffeng14}, and the best contacts, made of In, inject electrons over a barrier of a few tens of meV \cite{refhuang}. Hole conduction has been obtained in ambipolar devices with asymmetric contact metals \cite{reflin}, with van der Waals-integrated metal electrodes that make the polarity switchable \cite{refhu}, with transferred Pt contacts whose hole barrier approaches zero \cite{refwei}, and with vdW bottom contacts whose barriers approach the Schottky--Mott limit \cite{refkim24}. Complementary logic needs efficient hole injection, and how to obtain it from ordinary deposited metal contacts has received far less attention than electron injection \cite{refsong}.

Metal contacts to 2D semiconductors are governed by the Schottky barrier and its Fermi-level pinning, by the vdW gap between a deposited metal and the film, and by how current spreads from the contact into the channel \cite{refallain,refschulman,refwangchh}. Pinning arises from metal-induced gap states and from the bonding, defects and disorder at the interface \cite{reftung,refkim17}. The vdW gap adds a tunnel barrier \cite{refkang} but also weakens pinning \cite{refliusa}. Clean vdW contacts made by laminating metal films \cite{refliu18} or by depositing gold-capped indium \cite{refwangy19} approach the Schottky--Mott limit, semimetal contacts suppress gap states and reach contact resistances near the quantum limit \cite{refshen,refli23}, and clean high-work-function contacts give p-type transport in transition-metal dichalcogenides \cite{refwangy22}. For InSe, Chen \textit{et al.}\ measured a pinning factor $S=0.05$ for directly deposited metals and $S=0.5$ with an oxidized-monolayer interlayer \cite{refchen}. Calculations for ideal interfaces give $S=0.18$ for bulk InSe \cite{refguo} and 0.32 for monolayer InSe, with n-type contacts for the common metals \cite{refshi}. Transferred vdW contacts show a large barrier for Au and a negligible one for graphite \cite{refzhao}, so the interface depends strongly on how the contact is made.

Edge contacts remove the vdW gap and inject in-plane. They are established for graphene \cite{refwang} and have been made to MoS$_2$ \cite{refguim,refcheng,refjain}; in MoS$_2$ their resistance does not grow as the contact is shortened \cite{refcheng}. Ab initio transport calculations give a higher transmission for edge than for top contacts to MoS$_2$, but also a different barrier, because the interface chemistry differs \cite{refszabo,refparto}, and semiclassical models describe how carriers pass from the metal into the channel \cite{refarutchelvan}. In a multilayer film a top contact must also pass current across the layers, and the current path through the layers shifts with gate voltage \cite{refdasapp}. Real devices are gated, and the gate thins the Schottky barrier until tunnelling dominates injection, as in Schottky-barrier FETs \cite{refdas13,refpenu}. InSe is also strongly anisotropic: the out-of-plane mobility is estimated to be 500 times lower than the in-plane mobility \cite{refsangwan}, and the through-plane thermal conductivity is eleven times lower \cite{refrai}. Finally, 2D transistors on SiO$_2$ self-heat, limited by a thermal boundary conductance of about 14~MW\,m$^{-2}$K$^{-1}$ at the MoS$_2$/SiO$_2$ interface \cite{refyalon}. The device prospects and contact limits of 2D semiconductors are reviewed in Ref.~\cite{reffiori}.

Device simulations have compared the two geometries for MoS$_2$ with ab initio transport \cite{refszabo,refparto}, semiclassical injection models \cite{refarutchelvan}, WKB and quantum-transmitting-boundary calculations \cite{refdeylgat22,refdeylgat23,refdeylgat26} and TCAD of current crowding \cite{refsuryavanshi}. They find that the better geometry depends on doping and barrier height \cite{refdeylgat23}, on the anisotropy of the mobility \cite{refsuryavanshi}, and on the different charge-neutrality levels of edge and top interfaces, which can favour edge contacts for holes \cite{refparto}; ab initio transport finds hybrid edge--top contacts with a metal overlap of about 2~nm optimal for MoS$_2$ \cite{refpal}, and hybrid contacts raise the hole current of WSe$_2$ transistors 25-fold \cite{refchen25}. For InSe, published transistor simulations are, to our knowledge, ballistic quantum-transport studies of n-type channels, and all reported p-type devices use area contacts \cite{refhu,refwei,refkim24,refpasquale23,refseok}. None of these studies lets the effective mass itself be anisotropic. In a layered semiconductor the in-plane and out-of-plane masses can differ by an order of magnitude, and because tunnelling is exponentially sensitive to the mass along the path, a top contact, which tunnels across the layers, and an edge contact, which tunnels along them, face different effective barriers even at the same barrier height. InSe is an extreme case: its valence band is nearly flat in-plane \cite{refmag,refceferino} but dispersive along $c$. This paper asks which contact geometry gives the lowest resistance for hole injection into a few-layer InSe p-FET and why, and what the measured pinning of InSe contacts implies for p-type operation. We use a two-dimensional drift-diffusion model with a flux-balanced thermionic contact, nonlocal tunnelling with an anisotropic hole mass, transport and thermal anisotropy and a complete heat source, verify each ingredient, and benchmark the numerics on Au/MoS$_2$. The contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item a geometry phase diagram in the in-plane and out-of-plane carrier masses: top contacts win only when the mass ratio $m_\parallel/m_c$ exceeds a threshold that rises from 2--3 at a 1.05-eV barrier to 11--12 at 0.52~eV and is not reached at 0.25~eV; the boundary follows a line of constant mass ratio, set by the ratio of the WKB action integrals of the two barriers, a rule that applies to any layered semiconductor;
\item the consequence of the measured pinning: direct metal/InSe contacts have a hole barrier of about 1.1~eV regardless of the metal, so depinning must precede the choice of geometry;
\item tests of this comparison against the least-known model ingredients: the direction of tunnelling paths, unequal pinning strength and pinning level of the two geometries, and the hole mass, the last two in a Monte Carlo analysis;
\item open code and data that regenerate every quoted number.
\end{itemize}

\section{Model}
\label{sec:model}
\subsection{Device and band structure}
The two geometries are sketched in Fig.~\ref{fig:schem}. The device is an $N$-layer InSe film (0.83~nm per layer; $N=6$, $t=5$~nm by default) on 10~nm SiO$_2$ with a back gate (flat band at $V_G=0$), with a 50-nm channel. Top contacts of length $L_c$ cover the upper surface, and edge contacts cover the film's end faces. The band gap follows our fit to photoluminescence data \cite{refmudd,refmudd2013}, $E_g(N)=1.26+2.78N^{-1.70}$~eV, which gives 1.69~eV at 3 layers, 1.39~eV at 6 layers and 1.30~eV at 12 layers. 32\% of the confinement shift is assigned to the valence band, roughly $m_e/(m_e+m_h)$ with the $c$-axis masses; ARPES likewise finds the valence band nearly fixed in energy from four layers on \cite{refhamer}. The 6-layer film therefore has $E_V=5.00$~eV below vacuum. All parameters and their provenance are listed in Table~\ref{tab:params}. Unless stated otherwise, results use this \emph{reference alignment} with $S=1$, which isolates the effects of geometry, transport and tunnelling at a given barrier. Sections~\ref{sec:measured}--\ref{sec:pin}, and the results labelled `measured alignment', instead use the electron affinity $\chi=4.6$~eV adopted by Chen \textit{et al.}\ \cite{refchen}, which places $E_V$ of the 6-layer film at 5.90~eV, together with the measured and calculated pinning of InSe contacts. Calculated ionisation energies are lower, about 5.2~eV \cite{refguo}, so the absolute alignment is uncertain by several tenths of an eV; results are therefore also given versus the hole barrier $\phi_{Bp}$, which is the quantity that matters.

