\subsection{Hole-mass anisotropy decides the geometry}
\label{sec:mass}
The results so far use one hole mass, 0.17~$m_0$, for the density of states and for tunnelling in every direction. This is the bulk hole mass along the $c$ axis used by Mudd \textit{et al.}\ \cite{refmudd2013}; a GW-based fit gives an even lighter 0.078~$m_0$ \cite{refceferino}. It is the relevant mass for the vertical tunnelling paths of top contacts. In-plane holes are much heavier: the few-layer valence band is nearly flat \cite{refmag,refceferino}, and density-functional calculations give about 0.9~$m_0$ for six layers \cite{refpasquale,refpasquale23}. ARPES resolves the ring-shaped inversion of the band only in one and two layers and finds a direct gap from six layers \cite{refhamer}, and the calculated six-layer valence-band maximum lies only about 5~meV above $\Gamma$ \cite{refpasquale}. A parabolic in-plane mass of 0.9~$m_0$ is therefore a reasonable proxy at 300~K, although a GW-parametrized model shows that the band is not parabolic below ten layers \cite{refceferino}. Edge contacts tunnel in-plane and should see this heavier mass. Vertical transport in thick flakes gives a much heavier out-of-plane transport mass, 2.57~$m_0$ for electrons \cite{refsangwan}, which includes the interlayer barriers; Section~\ref{sec:phase} shows how the ranking changes if the $c$-axis tunnelling mass is that heavy.

A heavier isotropic mass raises both resistances and widens the edge advantage: with 0.5--2~$m_0$ for both geometries and parabolic dispersion, the top/edge ratio is 670--1800 for direct Pt, 310--750 with the interlayer and 57--98 for unpinned Pt, whether the heavier mass enters tunnelling alone or also the density of states (Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:mass}). The anisotropic mass changes the picture (Fig.~\ref{fig:mass}). With $m_c=0.17~m_0$, the two-band dispersion and the most favourable straight paths, the top/edge ratio falls as $m_\parallel$ rises, and falls faster at higher barriers. For barriers up to 0.52~eV (Pt with the interlayer) edge contacts remain better at every mass tested (0.3--1.2~$m_0$). At higher barriers the ranking reverses above a crossover mass of 0.86~$m_0$ at 0.6~eV and 0.42--0.44~$m_0$ at 1.0--1.05~eV. At the calculated 0.9~$m_0$, edge contacts are 11.5--12.5 times better at barriers of 0.2--0.25~eV and 2.1 times better with the interlayer, the geometries are equal at 0.6~eV (ratio 0.9), and top contacts are 20 times better at 1.0~eV and 27 times better with direct pinning. At high barriers all of the current tunnels, and a top contact tunnels along the light $c$ axis; at low barriers little tunnelling is needed, and the slow out-of-plane transport of top contacts dominates.

The model ingredients move the crossover by less than 0.2~$m_0$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:mass}b). With parabolic dispersion and normal paths the crossover masses are 0.77, 0.63, 0.43 and 0.42~$m_0$ at 0.52, 0.6, 1.0 and 1.05~eV; the two-band dispersion lowers them to 0.67, 0.54, 0.41 and 0.40~$m_0$, because it lowers the top-contact resistance at high barriers more than the edge-contact one. The most favourable straight paths raise them again at intermediate barriers, because an edge contact can then tunnel partly along the light $c$ axis. Two controls with the two-band dispersion at 0.9~$m_0$ isolate the cause (Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:mass}). With 0.9~$m_0$ in every direction, edge contacts win by 58--850 times at every barrier, and with the anisotropy swapped (heavy along $c$, light in-plane) by $1.8\times10^2$--$9\times10^6$, so the reversal comes from the light $c$-axis mass of top contacts, not from the direction of the gate field. A damaged edge interface works the other way: at barriers of 0.2--0.25~eV, top contacts win once the edge-interface transmission falls below 0.17--0.22 (normal paths).

Depinning matters even more with these masses. At $m_\parallel=0.9~m_0$ the interlayer lowers the Pt on-state resistance by $2.9\times10^2$ (top) and $1.7\times10^4$ (edge), against $2.7\times10^3$ and 700 with the light isotropic mass, and directly pinned contacts stay above $3\times10^4$~\ohmcm\ in either geometry.

The metal comparison of Sec.~\ref{sec:measured} changes accordingly (Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:anisometals}). With direct pinning the metal still hardly matters, with resistances 2.9$\times$ (edge) and 1.8$\times$ (top) apart from Pt to Ti, but top contacts are now 27--44 times better for every metal. After depinning the metal matters again, because it sets the barrier. With the oxide interlayer only Pt (0.52~eV) favours edge contacts (2.1$\times$); Pd and Au (0.79--0.80~eV) favour top contacts by 7.5--8$\times$, and Cr and Ti (1.10--1.18~eV) by 39--67$\times$. The calculated ideal pinning gives the same order (Pt: edge 2.1$\times$ better; Pd and Au: top 1.3--1.4$\times$; Cr and Ti: top 4--6$\times$), and an unpinned Pt contact favours edge contacts by 12.5$\times$. If the two interfaces are pinned differently, as calculations suggest for edge and top contacts \cite{refparto}, the difference in pinning outweighs the geometry (Supplementary Table~\ref{S-tab:seppin}): for Pt, a directly pinned edge contact loses to a depinned top contact by about 8000$\times$, an unpinned edge contact beats a directly pinned top contact by $5\times10^4$, and an unpinned edge contact beats a top contact with the interlayer by 190$\times$. Over the barrier sweep the top/edge ratio is 4.2 at $\phi_{Bp}=0$, peaks at 11.5 at 0.2~eV and falls to 0.90, 0.12 and 0.051 at 0.6, 0.8 and 1.0~eV (Fig.~\ref{fig:measured}b).

Two further approximations move the numbers but not the pattern (Supplementary Table~\ref{S-tab:physsens}). Applying the two-band form only along $c$, with a parabolic band in-plane, raises the edge-contact resistance at high barriers and lowers the top/edge ratio to 1.3 with the interlayer, 0.47 at 0.6~eV and 0.004--0.007 at 1.0--1.05~eV, while edge contacts still win by 11--12$\times$ at 0.2--0.25~eV. Adding a classical image-force lowering of the tunnelling barrier ($-q/16\pi\varepsilon d$, with $d$ the distance from the contact plane, no image in the oxide and a cut-off at 0.2~nm) favours edge contacts in 16 of 18 cases tested (Supplementary Table~\ref{S-tab:physsens}). With the isotropic InSe permittivity ($\varepsilon_r=6.4$) the ratio becomes 4.0 with the interlayer, 2.2 at 0.6~eV and 0.080--0.096 at 1.0--1.05~eV, so the crossover moves to between 0.6 and 1.0~eV. In a uniaxial medium a top contact (metal plane normal to $c$) sees the in-plane permittivity and an edge contact $\sqrt{\varepsilon_\parallel\varepsilon_c}$; with $\varepsilon_c=\varepsilon_\parallel/2$, as expected for weaker out-of-plane screening, the edge contact wins even at the measured direct pinning (by 4$\times$). Image-force lowering likewise improves side contacts to MoS$_2$ more than top contacts \cite{refdeylgat22}. Because the image potential enters only the tunnelling integral and not the drift-diffusion band edge, and its magnitude near the contact depends on the cut-off, these results are qualitative: they show that image-force lowering favours edge contacts and can change the ranking at high barriers, which a self-consistent treatment would need to quantify.

