\subsection{A geometry phase diagram in the two hole masses}
\label{sec:phase}
The $c$-axis mass is uncertain by more than an order of magnitude, from 0.078~$m_0$ (GW) and 0.17~$m_0$ (bulk) to the 2.57~$m_0$ out-of-plane transport mass of thick flakes \cite{refsangwan}. We therefore mapped the top/edge ratio over both masses, seven values of $m_c$ from 0.078 to 0.9~$m_0$ and six of $m_\parallel$ from 0.3 to 1.9~$m_0$, at four barriers, with the two-band dispersion and the most favourable straight paths (Fig.~\ref{fig:phase}; all 336 runs converged), and on a coarser grid up to $m_c=2.5~m_0$ (160 runs). Top contacts win only where holes are light along $c$ and heavy in-plane, and this region shrinks as the barrier falls. The mass ratio $m_\parallel/m_c$ at which the geometries are equal is 2.1--2.8 at 1.05~eV (direct pinning), 2.7--3.0 at 0.8~eV and 11.3--11.8 at the interlayer barrier (0.52~eV); at 0.25~eV edge contacts win at every ratio tested, up to 24. Over a factor of five in $m_c$ (0.12--0.6~$m_0$) the threshold ratio varies by less than $\pm15$\% at 1.05~eV and $\pm5$\% at 0.8~eV, so the boundary follows a line of constant mass ratio (dashed in Fig.~\ref{fig:phase}): the ratio, not either mass alone, decides the geometry. The WKB form of the transmission explains why. Each geometry's resistance is dominated by $\exp(2\kappa_0\sqrt{m}\,I)$, where $I=\int\sqrt{U}\,dx$ is the action integral of its barrier, so $\ln(R^{\rm top}/R^{\rm edge})\approx A+B\sqrt{m_c}-C\sqrt{m_\parallel}$ with $B/C=I^{\rm top}/I^{\rm edge}$. This three-parameter form fits each 42-point map with $R^2=0.90$--0.97, and $(B/C)^2$ gives thresholds of 2.3, 4.5, 9.0 and 32 at 1.05, 0.8, 0.52 and 0.25~eV, within a factor of 1.6 of the interpolated values (2.1--2.8, 2.7--3.0, 11.3--11.8 and above 24). The threshold ratio is therefore the square of the ratio of the barrier action integrals of the two contacts, $(I^{\rm top}/I^{\rm edge})^2$. It rises as the barrier falls ($I^{\rm top}/I^{\rm edge}=1.5$, 2.1, 3.0 and 5.7) because the gate thins the in-plane barrier of the edge contact, whereas the vertical path of the top contact keeps the vdW gap and the layers it must cross. A barrier-dependent ranking has been found before for MoS$_2$ contacts with an isotropic mass, with top contacts best at high barriers and hybrid contacts at low ones \cite{refdeylgat25}; the mass ratio is the second axis that this ranking needs in strongly anisotropic layered semiconductors. If the $c$-axis tunnelling mass were as heavy as the transport mass of thick flakes, edge contacts would win at every barrier, by 100--5000 times. Conversely, the GW band fits of Ref.~\cite{refceferino} give a flat, slightly inverted in-plane valence band for four to eight layers whose curvature is several times heavier than 0.9~$m_0$. Extending the map to $m_\parallel=3$ and 5.35~$m_0$ (the bulk GW in-plane mass) shows that the constant-ratio rule saturates at large ratios: the edge-contact resistance stops rising with $m_\parallel$, because its most favourable path tilts towards the light $c$ axis. At 0.52~eV the geometries then become nearly equal (top/edge ratio 1.03--1.09 with $m_c=0.17~m_0$; top contacts 1.3--1.5$\times$ better with $m_c=0.078~m_0$), at 0.25~eV edge contacts still win by 4.5--12$\times$ at a mass ratio of 69, and with direct pinning top contacts win by 28--150$\times$. A heavy in-plane mass therefore cannot make an edge contact arbitrarily poor; the threshold ratios quoted above hold up to ratios of about 10--20. The band masses of InSe ($m_\parallel/m_c=5.3$ with the bulk $c$-axis mass, 11.5 with the GW value) lie above the threshold at high barriers and below it at 0.25~eV, and at the threshold for the interlayer barrier: top contacts are 27 (bulk $m_c$) or 13 (GW $m_c$) times better with direct pinning, and edge contacts 12.5 or 5.2 times better for unpinned Pt and 2.1 or 1.0 times with the interlayer. The rule is not specific to InSe. In any layered semiconductor with light out-of-plane and heavy in-plane carriers, top contacts are favoured at high barriers and edge contacts after depinning, and the threshold ratio decides which applies to a given contact.

\paragraph{Hybrid contacts.} A metal that covers both the top face and the end of the film (Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:hybrid}) is never better, in this model, than the better of a pure edge and a pure top contact. With 50~nm of top overlap the hybrid contact behaves like a top contact: within 2--4\% of it at 0.6--1.05~eV and 1.6--1.9$\times$ worse than the edge contact at 0.2--0.52~eV. We attribute this to the end face lying 50~nm from the channel, in a region whose potential is fixed by the overlying metal rather than modulated by the back gate, so its barrier is not thinned. Shortening the overlap to 2~nm brings the hybrid contact within 13--16\% of the edge contact at 0.2--0.25~eV, but at high barriers it then loses the top-face area and is 2.7--2.8$\times$ worse than the edge contact and 54--78$\times$ worse than the top contact at 1.0--1.05~eV. No overlap between 2 and 50~nm is within 50\% of the better geometry at every barrier. This differs from \emph{ab initio} transport for MoS$_2$, which finds a 2-nm overlap optimal \cite{refpal}; that calculation includes the bonding at the metal edge and the different pinning of edge and top interfaces \cite{refparto}, which our model represents only through a common pinning factor, and an electrostatic model of MoS$_2$ contacts that includes metal-induced gap states finds hybrid contacts best only at low barriers and top contacts best at high ones \cite{refdeylgat25}, as here; and the 25-fold gain measured on WSe$_2$ \cite{refchen25} also reflects fabrication and pinning that this comparison does not model.

\paragraph{Bottom contacts.} With the metal under the film, as in the Pt bottom contacts of Ref.~\cite{refseok}, a back gate is screened from the contact interface (Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:bottom}). The bottom contact then loses the gate-induced thinning that a top contact still receives through the 5-nm film, and with the anisotropic mass it is 10--11$\times$ worse than the top contact at 0.2--0.25~eV, 27$\times$ with the interlayer and 260--350$\times$ at 1.0--1.05~eV; with the light isotropic mass the penalty is larger still (108--$3\times10^4\times$ where the runs converged). In a back-gated device bottom contacts are therefore the worst of the four geometries; they become competitive only with a gate that also acts on the contact region, such as a top gate or a gate under thin hBN that extends beneath the metal.

