\subsection{Self-heating}
\label{sec:heat}
Self-heating is computed with the complete heat source of Sec.~\ref{sec:model} and is detailed in Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:heat}. At the reference alignment and the same gated operating point ($\Phi_M=5.0$~eV, $V_G=-2$~V, $V_D=1$~V) the edge-contacted device carries 3.7~A/cm and heats by 150~K, and the 50-nm top-contacted device carries 1.16~A/cm and heats by 24~K. Per unit power the edge device is also about twice as hot (41 vs 21~K\,cm/W), because its footprint on the oxide is a third of that of the top-contacted film. At the measured alignment self-heating depends on the pinning: directly pinned contacts heat the edge device by only 0.2~K, the oxide interlayer by 24~K, and unpinned Pt contacts by 128~K. The lattice reaches 63\% of its final rise within 0.19--0.25~ns with or without tunnelling, so DC temperatures apply to pulses longer than about a nanosecond. Temperatures carry an uncertainty of about $\pm3$~K from the energy balance and the contact Peltier constant. They exclude the thermal boundary resistance at the InSe/SiO$_2$ interface, which for MoS$_2$/SiO$_2$ is about $7\times10^{-8}$~m$^2$K/W \cite{refyalon}, seven times the gate-side value used here; a three times larger gate-side resistance already raises the rises by 58--73\%, so the temperatures of depinned edge-contacted devices are probably underestimated. These numbers use the light isotropic mass. With the anisotropic mass (Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:anisoheat}) the edge device still carries more current at the reference point (4.1 against 2.0~A/cm at 1~V) and heats by 172~K against 34~K; at the measured alignment the interlayer heats the edge device by 14~K and the top device by 1~K, and unpinned Pt contacts by 126 and 10~K. Edge-contacted devices remain the hotter ones wherever the barrier is low enough for them to carry more current.

\section{Discussion and conclusions}
\label{sec:concl}
For hole injection into few-layer InSe p-FETs the simulations give six design rules:
\begin{enumerate}
\item With the measured pinning of direct metal/InSe contacts ($S=0.05$), every common metal gives a hole barrier of about 1.1~eV and an electron-injecting contact. Hole operation then relies entirely on gate-induced tunnelling, and the metal work function barely matters (2--3$\times$ from Ti to Pt).
\item The barrier must first be lowered by depinning: the measured oxide interlayer brings Pt to 0.52~eV and lowers the on-state resistance by $3\times10^2$ to $2\times10^4$, depending on geometry and model.
\item Which geometry is better depends on the barrier left after depinning, the hole-mass anisotropy and the film thickness; top contacts win only when $m_\parallel/m_c$ exceeds 2--3 at 0.8--1.05~eV and 11--12 at 0.52~eV. With the calculated in-plane mass, edge contacts are 2--12 times better for Pt after depinning (barriers up to 0.52~eV), the geometries are equal near 0.6~eV, and top contacts are 27--44 times better for every metal at the measured direct pinning, because they tunnel along the light $c$ axis. Pd and Au, whose barrier stays near 0.8~eV with the interlayer, favour top contacts. With a light or isotropic mass edge contacts would win at every barrier. In ungated, barrier-limited operation, stronger pinning of edge contacts also reverses the ranking, and a damaged edge interface (transmission below about 0.2) does so at low barriers.
\item Nonlocal tunnelling is essential: thermionic and local enhancement models underestimate the on-current by orders of magnitude.
\item With the interlayer and the calculated in-plane mass, top contacts are better only near four layers and edge contacts in films of six or more layers. Edge-contacted devices carry more current and need heat removal once the contacts are depinned.
\item A hybrid contact that covers both the top face and the end of the film is no better than the better of the two pure geometries: with a long overlap it behaves as a top contact, and with a 2-nm overlap it approaches the edge contact at low barriers but loses the top-face area at high ones. Bottom contacts under a back gate are 10--350 times worse than top contacts, because the metal screens the gate from the contact interface.
\end{enumerate}
The p-type InSe devices that reach low hole barriers all use depinned area contacts: van der Waals bottom contacts with barriers near the Schottky--Mott limit \cite{refkim24}, Pt bottom contacts that inject holes down to 1.5~K \cite{refseok} and transferred Pt contacts \cite{refwei}. This is the regime in which the model predicts edge contacts to be 2--12 times better if holes are light along $c$; no experiment has yet compared the two geometries on the same film. In the units used for transistors, the simulated on-state resistance (both contacts and a 50-nm channel, $V_G=-3$~V on 10~nm of SiO$_2$) with the anisotropic mass is 560~k$\Omega\,\mu$m (edge) and 1.2~M$\Omega\,\mu$m (top) for Pt with the interlayer, and 6.1 and 76~k$\Omega\,\mu$m for unpinned Pt. No hole contact resistance of InSe has been measured, so these are predictions that a transfer-length measurement on depinned contacts could test. A compact transmission-line model reproduces the simulated top-contact resistance with a median error of 11\% when tunnelling is weak (Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:compact}).

The results are conditional on inputs that have not been measured for InSe. The in-plane hole mass is known only from calculation, and at barriers above about 0.5~eV it decides which geometry wins (Sec.~\ref{sec:mass}). The absolute band alignment of few-layer InSe is uncertain by several tenths of an eV, and the pinning of edge contacts, which may differ from that of top contacts in both strength and level \cite{refparto}, has not been measured. The vdW-gap transmission and the interface thermal resistances are assumed. The band gap is fitted to photoluminescence, which gives the optical gap, and the bulk-like statistics ignore subband quantization in the 5-nm film. The contact Peltier coefficient and the drift-diffusion flux differ by $kT/2$ per carrier, which changes temperatures by up to 3~K; a generalized Scharfetter--Gummel scheme based on the Kelvin formula for the Seebeck coefficient would remove this inconsistency \cite{refkantner}. Edge contacts are given a perfect interface transmission; etch damage, voids or unpassivated edge states would lower it. The one-dimensional WKB treatment is checked against quadrature on the same band profile but not against a two-dimensional quantum calculation such as those of Refs.~\cite{refszabo,refparto}; with the anisotropic mass, choosing the most favourable straight path instead of the contact normal changes the top/edge ratios by up to a factor of 40 at the heaviest in-plane mass, so the path geometry matters. The in-plane valence band is not parabolic \cite{refceferino}, and the effective in-plane tunnelling mass used here is only a proxy for it. A two-dimensional quantum-transport calculation of both contacts with the anisotropic band structure would be the definitive test of the path geometry and of the in-plane band; it is beyond the scope of this drift-diffusion study. Image-force lowering, tested as a sensitivity case, favours edge contacts further, by an amount that depends strongly on the out-of-plane permittivity and the cut-off (Sec.~\ref{sec:mass}). The bottom-contact geometry treats the oxide edge next to the metal as a free surface and neglects any fringing field of the gate at the metal edge. The comparison with Chen \textit{et al.}\ tests only the barrier extraction, because their on-currents were measured at 2~K. The most useful experiments would measure the in-plane hole mass of few-layer InSe and the contact resistance of edge and top contacts on the same film, before and after depinning.

